f Experimental Psychology</source><year>1935</year><volume>28</volume><fpage>643</fpage><lpage>662</lpage></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Stroop, J. R. (1935). Studies of interference in serial verbal reactions. Journal of Experimental Psychology,28, 643&#x02013;662. </citation></ref><ref id="CR124"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Thorndike</surname><given-names>EL</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Ideo-motoraction</article-title><source>PsychologicalReview</source><year>1913</year><volume>20</volume><fpage>91</fpage><lpage>106</lpage></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Thorndike, E. L. (1913). Ideo-motor action. Psychological Review, 20, 91&#x02013;106. </citation></ref><ref id="CR110"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Treisman</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Features and objects: The Fourteenth Bartlett Memorial Lecture</article-title><source>Quarterly Journal of Experimental Psychology</source><year>1988</year><volume>40A</volume><fpage>201</fpage><lpage>237</lpage></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Treisman, A. (1988). Features and objects: The Fourteenth Bartlett Memorial Lecture. Quarterly Journal of Experimental Psychology,40A, 201&#x02013;237. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">3406448</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR111"><citation citation-type="book"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Umilt&#x000e0;</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Nicoletti</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><person-group person-group-type="editor"><name><surname>Alegria</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Holender</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Jun&#x000e7;a Morais</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Radeau</su