J. F., &#x00026; Denier van der Gon, J. J. (1991). Reaction-time-dependent differences in the initial movement direction of fast goal-directed arm movements. Human Movement Science,10, 713&#x02013;726. </citation></ref><ref id="CR114"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>der Malsburg</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The what and why of binding: The modeler&#x02019;s perspective</article-title><source>Neuron</source><year>1999</year><volume>24</volume><fpage>95</fpage><lpage>104</lpage></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Von der Malsburg, C. (1999). The what and why of binding: The modeler&#x02019;s perspective. Neuron,24, 95&#x02013;104. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10677030</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR115"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wallace</surname><given-names>RA</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>S-R compatibility and the idea of a response code</article-title><source>Journal of Experimental Psychology</source><year>1971</year><volume>88</volume><fpage>354</fpage><lpage>360</lpage></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Wallace, R. A. (1971). S-R compatibility and the idea of a response code. Journal of Experimental Psychology,88, 354&#x02013;360. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">5090926</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR116"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Waszak</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Herwig</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Effect anticipation modulates deviance processing in the brain</article-title><source>Brain Research</source><year>2007</year><volume>1183</volume><fpage>74</fpage><lpage>82</lpage></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Waszak, F., &#x00026; Herwig, A. (2007). Effect anticipation modulates deviance processing in the brain. Brain Research,1183, 74&#x02013;82. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17927968</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR117"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Waszak</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><name><surname>Hommel</surname><given-names>B</given-names></name><name><surname>Allport</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Task-switching and long-term priming: Role of episodic stimulus-task bindings in task-shift costs</article-title><source>Cognitive Psychology</source><year>2003</year><volume>46</volume><fpage>361</fpage><lpage>413</lpage></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Waszak, F., Hommel, B., &#x00026; Allport, A. (2003). Task-switching and long-term priming: Role of episodic stimulus-task bindings in task-shift costs. Cognitive Psychology,46, 361&#x02013;413. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12809680</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR118"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Welford</surname><given-names>AT</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The &#x0201c;psychological refractory period&#x0201d; and the timing of high-speed performance&#x02014;A review and a theory</article-title><source>British Journal of Psychology</source><year>1952</year><volume>43</volume><fpage>2</fpage><lpage>19</lpage></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Welford, A. T. (1952). The &#x0201c;psychological refractory period&#x0201d; and the timing of high-speed performance&#x02014;A review and a theory. British Journal of Psychology,43, 2&#x02013;19. </citation></ref><ref id="CR119"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wenke</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Gaschler</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Nattkemper</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Instruction-induced feature binding</article-title><source>Psychological Research</source><year>2005</year><volume>71</volume><fpage>92</fpage><lpage>106</lpage></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Wenke, D., Gaschler, R., &#x00026; Nattkemper, D. (2005). Instruction-induced feature binding. Psychological Research,71, 92&#x02013;106. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16341902</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR120"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wiediger</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Fournier</surname><given-names>LR</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>An action sequence withheld in memory can delay execution of visually guided actions: The generalization of response compatibility interference</article-title><source>Journal of Experimental Psychology: Human Perception and Performance</source><year>2008</year><volume>34</volume><fpage>1136</fpage><lpage>1149</lpage></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Wiediger, M., &#x00026; Fournier, L. R. (2008). An action sequence withheld in memory can delay execution of visually guided actions: The generalization of response compatibility interference. Journal of Experimental Psychology: Human Perception and Performance,34, 1136&#x02013;1149. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18823201</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR121"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wolpert</surname><given-names>DM</given-names></name><name><surname>Ghahramani</surname><given-names>Z</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Computational principles of movement neuroscience</article-title><source>Nature Neuroscience</source><year>2000</year><volume>3</volume><fpage>1212</fpage><lpage>1217</lpage></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Wolpert, D. M., &#x00026; Ghahramani, Z. (2000). Computational principles of movement neuroscience. Nature Neuroscience,3, 1212&#x02013;1217. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11127840</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="CR122"><citation citation-type="other">Wykowska, A., Schub&#x000f6;, A., &#x00026; Hommel, B. (2008). How you move is what you see: Action planning biases selection in visual search (Submitted).</citation></ref><ref id="CR123"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wylie</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Allport</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Task switching and the measurement of &#x0201c;switch costs&#x0201d;</article-title><source>Psychological Research</source><year>2000</year><volume>63</volume><fpage>212</fpage><lpage>233</lpage></citation><citation citation-type="display-unstructured">Wylie, G., &#x00026; Allport, A. (2000). Task switching and the measurement of &#x0201c;switch costs&#x0201d;. Psychological Research,63, 212&#x02013;233. <pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11004877</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list><fn-group><fn id="Fn1"><label>1</label><p>From these considerations it should be clear that TEC considers action plans less complete than other approaches do. TEC agrees with feedforward control models (see Jordan &#x00026; Wolpert, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR57">1999</xref>) that actions are, or at least can be planned ahead of time but disagrees with the frequent assumption that this kind of planning is sufficient to specify all the parameters needed and to specify them as precisely as needed&#x02014;with the possible exception of very simple, completely ballistic movements.</p></fn><fn id="Fn2"><label>2</label><p>Even though features from task-relevant dimensions have consistently been observed to produce considerably stronger and more reliable effects, features of task-irrelevant dimensions do not seem to be entirely ineffective (e.g., Hommel, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR43">1998a</xref>; Hommel &#x00026; Colzato, 2004), even though their impact is sometimes only transient and disappears with increasing practice (Colzato, Raffone &#x00026; Hommel, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR13">2006</xref>). It is possible that nominally irrelevant features are top-down primed by processing strategies (e.g., to weight and process all features of a stimulus equally in the beginning of a task and to focus attention on relevant information with increasing expertise) and chronic attention to particular dimensions, and that particularly salient features attract attention in a bottom-up fashion. Thus, intentional weighting seems to bias processing towards relevant information but does not seem to exclude irrelevant information altogether.</p></fn><fn id="Fn3"><label>3</label><p>This logic has recently been extended to affective features of stimuli and responses (Eder &#x00026; Rothermund, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR21">2008</xref>; Lavender &#x00026; Hommel, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR70">2007</xref>), which allows an account of affective stimulus-response interactions (e.g., Beckers, De Houwer &#x00026; Eelen, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR6">2002</xref>; De Houwer &#x00026; Eelen, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR16">1998</xref>) in terms of TEC.</p></fn><fn id="Fn4"><label>4</label><p>Integrating and thereby temporarily occupying feature codes may also prevent actions from triggering themselves in a vicious circle. Carrying out an action produces action effects and perceiving action effects is assumed to prime the corresponding action, which implies that every action could keep priming itself over and over again (MacKay, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR75">1986</xref>)&#x02014;a phenomenon that is indeed observed in infants and known as the <italic>circular reflex</italic>. The more an agent learns to predict the effects of his or her actions, the more the codes of these effects will become part of the corresponding action plans. Integrating these codes will prevent them from responding to the experience of the eventually produced effects, which effectively will prevent the plan from being activated by them a second time. The same mechanism may also be responsible for why we can&#x02019;t tickle ourselves (Blakemore, Wolpert &#x00026; Frith, <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR7">1998</xref>).</p></fn></fn-group></back></article>